body:
OK, looking at the mentioned code I see:

```
                        if (canUpdateInPlace(newRepositoryMetadata, existing)) {
                            if (repositoryMetadata.settings().equals(newRepositoryMetadata.settings())) {
                                // Previous version is the same as this one no update is needed.
                                return currentState;
                            }
                            // we're updating in place so the updated metadata must point at the same uuid and generations
                            updatedMetadata = repositoryMetadata.withSettings(newRepositoryMetadata.settings());
                        } else {
                            ensureRepositoryNotInUse(currentState, request.name());
                            updatedMetadata = newRepositoryMetadata;
                        }
```

I think solving this issue would be just extending `if (repositoryMetadata.settings().equals(newRepositoryMetadata.settings())) {` to check it is also not corrupted.

And solving related issue https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/issues/89696 would be to add `ensureNoSearchableSnapshotsIndicesInUse()` below the `ensureRepositoryNotInUse()` line, to consider if there are any mounted searchable snapshots using the repos. cc @fcofdez
